    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.12",
    "topic_title": "Sampling Distributions and the Central Limit Theorem",
    "stem": "A population of waiting times is strongly right-skewed. For each sample size n, a researcher considers the mean waiting time from every possible random sample of size n, drawn with replacement. According to the central limit theorem, what happens as n increases?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The population distribution of individual waiting times becomes more nearly normal.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Population-normality trap: Increasing sample size does not change the population’s distribution. The central limit theorem describes the distribution of sample means, not individual waiting times. Fix: Identify whether each plotted value is an individual observation or a sample mean."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The sampling distribution of the sample mean becomes more nearly normal.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Each value in this distribution represents a whole sample’s mean, not an individual waiting time. The central limit theorem says this distribution becomes approximately normal as sample size increases, even when the population is skewed."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The distribution of individual waiting times within a single sample becomes more nearly normal.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Single-sample normality trap: The central limit theorem does not make the observations within one sample normally distributed. A large random sample can still reflect the population’s strong right skew. Fix: Check whether the distribution summarizes one sample’s observations or means from all possible samples."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The sampling distribution of the sample mean must have the same shape as the population for every sample size.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Shape-inheritance trap: Sample means need not have the same distributional shape as individual observations. Averaging increasingly many observations produces an approximately normal sampling distribution under the central limit theorem. Fix: Separate the distribution of individual observations from the distribution of whole samples’ means."
     }
    ]
   },
