   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.12",
    "topic_title": "Sampling Distributions and the Central Limit Theorem",
    "stem": "Household incomes in a large city are strongly right-skewed. A researcher takes 1,000 random samples, each of 60 households, computes the mean income of each sample, and makes a dotplot of the 1,000 sample means. Which statement best describes this dotplot?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The dots form a strongly right-skewed shape, matching the population, because random samples resemble the population they come from.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses the sampling distribution with the population or a single sample's data. Individual samples of 60 may look skewed, but their means pile up in an approximately normal shape as sample size grows. Fix: Ask what one dot represents before predicting the shape of the dotplot."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Each dot is one household's income, and the dots are approximately normal because the researcher took 1,000 samples.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misidentifies each dot as an individual and credits the number of samples for normality. Each dot is a sample mean, and the shape depends on the size of each sample, not how many samples were drawn. Fix: Check whether normality comes from n per sample, not the count of samples."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Each dot is one sample's mean income, and the dots form an approximately normal shape because each sample has a fairly large size of 60.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The dotplot is a simulated sampling distribution of the sample mean, so each dot summarizes a whole sample of 60 households. The Central Limit Theorem says this distribution is approximately normal for large samples, even though the population of incomes is skewed."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The dots are approximately normal because the Central Limit Theorem makes the population of incomes approximately normal when samples are large.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This applies the Central Limit Theorem to the population. Sampling never changes the population; incomes stay right-skewed, and only the distribution of sample means becomes approximately normal. Fix: Attach the normality claim to the distribution of the statistic, never to the population."
     }
    ]
   },
