    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.12",
    "topic_title": "Sampling Distributions and the Central Limit Theorem",
    "stem": "A population of delivery times is strongly right-skewed and has a finite mean and standard deviation. For a chosen sample size n, a researcher repeatedly takes independent random samples and records each sample’s mean delivery time. Which statement correctly applies the Central Limit Theorem to this process?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "As n increases, the population distribution of delivery times becomes more nearly normal.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Population-normality trap: Increasing sample size does not change the population of delivery times. The Central Limit Theorem describes the distribution of sample means, not individual delivery times. Fix: Identify whether each plotted value represents an individual or a whole sample’s mean."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "As n increases, the distribution of individual delivery times within each sample becomes more nearly normal.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sample-data normality trap: The Central Limit Theorem does not make individual observations within a sample normally distributed. Those observations still come from the right-skewed population. Fix: Separate the distribution within one sample from the distribution of means across samples."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "With n fixed at 1, increasing the number of repeated samples makes the distribution of recorded sample means more nearly normal.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Repetition-versus-size trap: With n = 1, each sample mean is one individual delivery time, so its distribution matches the right-skewed population. More repetitions do not increase the size of each sample. Fix: Check whether the change increases observations per sample or only the number of samples."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "As n increases, the sampling distribution of the sample mean becomes more nearly normal, even though the population remains right-skewed.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Each recorded value summarizes a whole sample, so these values represent a sampling distribution of the mean. The Central Limit Theorem concerns this distribution’s shape as sample size increases, not the population’s shape."
     }
    ]
   },
